<p>The shop’s customer-history query returns 90 orders. After delivery notes grow, it still returns those same identifiers and totals, but a scan touches over twenty times as many table pages. The extra work is in data the query does not return.</p>
<p>A table’s physical organisation explains how that can happen. Rows share pages, pages share a cache, and old row versions can outlive the values an application sees. Follow the orders through wider notes, retained versions and a monthly layout to distinguish work worth removing from space worth reusing.</p>
<p>First complete <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/">Understanding and improving database performance</a>, including <a href="/review/relational-next/performance/#change-one-explanation-into-a-measured-improvement">its comparison method</a>. This investigation uses that method for physical work. PostgreSQL 18.6 provides the captured execution; the small page drawings are explicitly labelled models. InnoDB comparisons describe MySQL 8.4 documentation, without a MySQL measurement.</p>
