<h2 id="a-row-request-becomes-page-work">A row request becomes page work</h2>
<p>A <strong>page</strong> is a fixed-size unit of storage organisation and buffering. Ordinary PostgreSQL heap pages are 8 KiB in this server. A page holds several row versions plus bookkeeping and free space; requesting one row can require bringing its whole page into the database’s memory cache.</p>
<Sql code={lookup} label="The same identifiers and totals before and after the notes grow" />
<p>Our fixture has 9,000 orders and 100 customers, with 90 orders each. There is an index on the order identifier, but none on customer. Both versions of this query use a sequential scan followed by a small sort. The scan tests every visible row to discover the 90 matches. Its table-page work follows the stored table, rather than the answer’s size.</p>
<p>With 40-character notes, the table occupies 103 heap pages. With 1,800-character notes, the copy occupies 2,250. Both store these notes inline: the fixture explicitly prevents compression and out-of-line storage to isolate width. Captured warm executions report 103 and 2,250 shared-buffer hits respectively, with zero shared-buffer reads. The ordered identifiers and totals match exactly. This establishes extra page processing, without establishing a device-I/O or latency increase.</p>
<p>Figure 1 reduces the table to twelve invented orders. Compare short notes with wide notes, then select identifiers alone. Watch the page count rather than the answer count.</p>
<StorageFigure scene="width" number={1} />
<p>Keep four quantities separate. <strong>Rows returned</strong> describe the answer. <strong>Pages examined</strong> describe storage work. A <strong>buffer hit</strong> means the database already held the page. A PostgreSQL <strong>shared-buffer read</strong> loads a page into that cache, but the operating system’s file cache may supply it without a device read. A hit still requires finding the page, checking visibility and processing rows.</p>
<p><code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS, TIMING OFF)</code> executes the query to collect this evidence. Buffers are accesses, so repeated visits can count the same page repeatedly; parent-node totals include child work and must not be added down the plan. Here a single scan visits each heap page once. <a href="/review/relational-next/query-plans/">Diagnosing a query from its execution plan</a> owns the fuller reporting conventions and access-path investigation.</p>

<h2 id="where-a-row-lives-determines-locality">Where a row lives determines locality</h2>
<p><strong>Locality</strong> means that data used together is stored close enough to share work. Four wanted orders on one page need fewer distinct table pages than four orders on four pages. That difference matters even when the matching row count and the index are unchanged.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL normally stores rows in a <strong>heap</strong>, choosing space without maintaining primary-key order. An index’s row locator identifies a heap page and an item on it. PostgreSQL exposes that physical location as <code>ctid</code>, such as <code>(12,3)</code>. It identifies a row version’s location, not a lasting business identity: updates and rewrites can change it.</p>
<p>Figure 2 begins after the row locators have been found. Step through the four table visits, then compare nearby placement. Finally choose a scan, which must test every page because it lacks those locators.</p>
<StorageFigure scene="locality" number={2} />
<p>The scattered route touches four different pages. Nearby matches share one page, though the model still counts four row-driven page accesses. With pages already cached, neither route loads a table page, but their processing remains. The model assumes enough cache for the six drawn pages and omits index work; it is not an execution plan.</p>
<p>InnoDB organises table rows inside a <strong>clustered index</strong>, normally by primary key. A secondary index carries that key as its row locator, and the engine uses it to find the table row. Nearby clustered keys can share leaf pages; a secondary key’s neighbours need not be neighbouring table rows. Key order is a logical ordering through the tree, not a promise of contiguous filesystem blocks. SQL result order still needs <code>ORDER BY</code>.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL’s <code>CLUSTER</code> can reorder a heap once; later changes do not maintain that ordering. Whether a new index, coverage or a different key removes enough table visits is the question for <a href="/review/relational-next/indexes/">Choosing indexes for a workload</a>. Physical proximity alone cannot establish which route the planner will choose.</p>

<h2 id="the-working-set-includes-more-than-the-returned-columns">The working set includes more than the returned columns</h2>
<p>The <strong>working set</strong> is the set of pages repeatedly needed by a workload over the interval that matters. It includes table pages, relevant index pages, visibility information and any large-value pages the executions actually touch. It differs from both the whole database and the few columns returned to a caller.</p>
<p>The wide-note scan repeatedly needs 2,250 pages instead of 103. If competing work evicts those pages before their next use, more visits require loading them again. Wider inline rows, deliberate spare capacity, unreclaimed versions and poor locality can all spread useful rows over more pages. A high hit ratio can hide a large amount of repeated processing, while one broad scan can have a different cache pattern from repeated lookups. Concurrent memory competition and sizing belong with <a href="/review/relational-next/concurrent-load/">performance under concurrent load</a>.</p>
<p>Changing this fixture’s query to select only <code>id</code> left the warm scan at 2,250 hits. Projection reduced the output, but did not repack the existing table. Separating rarely needed inline notes into a related table could make the frequently scanned rows narrower; it also adds a relationship, another access when notes are needed and write obligations. Test that complete cost rather than assuming a vertical split helps every query.</p>
<p>Large values can already have a separate physical home. PostgreSQL’s <strong>TOAST</strong> mechanism may compress a large variable-length value, move it to chunk rows in an associated table, or do both. The main row then holds either its inline representation or an out-of-line pointer. A logical value’s character count does not tell you which representation was stored.</p>
<p>Figure 3 uses a second fixture with 1,000 orders and 8,192-byte notes. It explicitly allows out-of-line storage and disables compression, so fetching the complete note has a visible separate cost. Compare the identifier-only aggregate with an aggregate that hashes each whole note.</p>
<StorageFigure scene="toast" number={3} />
<p>The identifier aggregate used 7 shared hits; consuming every complete note used 4,008. Both reported zero shared reads. The main heap was only 56 KiB, whereas total allocated storage, including its associated large-value storage and indexes, was about 8.34 MiB. This is why a heap-only size and a total size answer different questions.</p>
<p>Reading a pointer does not automatically fetch its value. Fetching or sending a full value generally needs its stored chunks and, if compressed, decompression. Some operations can use metadata or part of a value, so a test such as length alone may fail to exercise the work you intended. The full-note hash deliberately consumes the contents. An update that leaves an out-of-line value unchanged normally preserves that value rather than copying its chunks.</p>

<h2 id="updates-leave-work-behind-them">Updates leave work behind them</h2>
<p>An order changes from total 10 to 11 while an earlier report is still reading. PostgreSQL creates a new heap row version and retains the earlier one while a relevant snapshot may need it. A committed deletion similarly stops the row appearing in later results without necessarily making its stored bytes immediately reusable. <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">Making concurrent changes correct</a> explains which snapshots see which changes; here the consequence is physical retention.</p>
<p>Play Figure 4 with the earlier reader held open. Then let that reader finish and replay the cleanup step.</p>
<StorageFigure scene="versions" number={4} />
<p>An update also creates write work: changes to heap pages, possible index changes and write-ahead log records. Changed pages become <strong>dirty</strong> in memory and must eventually be written; log generation and page flushing are separate obligations. A buffer counter cannot establish what an acknowledged commit survives. <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">Transaction design</a> owns the commit contract; <a href="/review/relational-next/maintenance-and-upgrades/">service maintenance</a> owns ongoing log and flushing obligations, with restoration in <a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/">Backing up and restoring relational data</a>.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL can avoid new ordinary index entries with a <strong>HOT update</strong> when the changed columns are not referenced by those indexes and the new version fits on the old version’s page. Summarising indexes such as BRIN have a special exception. Leaving spare space through table fillfactor can improve that opportunity, while increasing the pages needed for scans. Free space is therefore sometimes useful capacity, not automatically waste.</p>
<p>In a separate 2,000-order fixture, we established a repeatable-read snapshot, updated every total and deleted every fourth order. The earlier reader still saw 2,000 rows totalling 20,000; the later reader saw 1,500 totalling 16,500. The update generated 647,809 WAL bytes in this run and reported 62 shared blocks dirtied. Those counts are neither the number of row versions nor the eventual number of device writes.</p>
<p>The heap grew from 77 to 134 pages. Cleanup while the snapshot remained could not remove the versions that reader needed. After the reader ended and cleanup ran again, reusable free space rose from 118,512 to 718,576 bytes. The heap stayed at 134 pages: this experiment disabled end-of-file truncation to isolate reuse. Inserting 500 replacement rows then reused space without growing the heap, although its primary-key index grew by one page.</p>
<p>InnoDB keeps the reconstruction information for older reads in undo records rather than putting PostgreSQL-style complete old row versions beside the current heap row. Purge discards eligible history and delete-marked records after readers no longer need them. A long snapshot can therefore retain undo history and related work even when the current table’s row count is stable. Looking only for PostgreSQL-style heap dead tuples would miss that explanation.</p>

<h2 id="decide-whether-layout-and-cleanup-explain-the-cost">Decide whether layout and cleanup explain the cost</h2>
<p>A large file and a slow query are two observations, not proof that rebuilding the file will help. First ask which pages the query touches and why those pages exist. A cold, rarely read history region can occupy much of the file without belonging to the hot working set.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Separate physical explanations"><table><thead><tr><th>Hypothesis</th><th>Discriminating evidence</th><th>Intervention to test</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Inline rows became wider</td><td>Stored representation, heap pages and same-route buffer work increased; visible rows did not</td><td>A narrower hot representation, or an access route avoiding those pages</td></tr>
<tr><td>Matching rows lost locality</td><td>The same matching identifiers now occupy more heap pages</td><td>A layout that keeps frequently co-accessed rows together</td></tr>
<tr><td>Old snapshots retain versions</td><td>Old transaction horizons, retained physical versions and cleanup that cannot yet reclaim them</td><td>Shorten unnecessary transaction lifetimes, then allow cleanup</td></tr>
<tr><td>Eligible cleanup falls behind</td><td>Dead-version growth across intervals, cleanup progress and resource or blocking evidence</td><td>Restore adequate cleanup within the service budget</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reusable gaps remain</td><td>Free bytes within pages after cleanup, without matching file shrinkage</td><td>Observe reuse under expected growth before paying for compaction</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Start with inexpensive PostgreSQL relation sizes and activity/statistics observations. <code>pg_relation_size</code> measures the named relation’s main file by default; <code>pg_total_relation_size</code> also includes indexes and associated TOAST storage. Approximate dead-row statistics are useful trends, not a precise allocation map. An old transaction alone does not prove it currently holds a relevant snapshot: inspect transaction and snapshot horizons, including other engine-supported retention sources.</p>
<p>For a consequential case, <code>pgstattuple</code> scans the relation to report tuple and free-space information. Its full scan costs I/O and can disturb cache conditions; concurrent changes mean it is not an instantaneous whole-table snapshot. Its “dead” classification does not prove every reported byte is already removable by vacuum: our held-snapshot capture reported 2,500 dead tuples while needed versions remained. <code>pgstattuple_approx</code> can skip suitable pages using visibility information, trading some exact values for estimates. Obtain the necessary privileges and choose the scope before scanning a large live relation.</p>
<p>Be precise about fragmentation. <strong>Internal free space</strong> is room inside allocated pages. <strong>Lost locality</strong> spreads related rows across more pages. <strong>External fragmentation</strong> concerns how files or extents are placed by the storage system; a heap free-space percentage does not measure it. One “bloat” number cannot distinguish all three or predict a query’s benefit from reorganisation.</p>

<h3 id="reorganisation-exchanges-future-work-for-immediate-work">Reorganisation exchanges future work for immediate work</h3>
<p>Ordinary PostgreSQL vacuum makes eligible space reusable and can sometimes truncate wholly empty pages at the file’s end. It does not generally pack surviving rows together throughout the file. A rewrite can compact them; an ordered rewrite can also improve locality. Those are different predicted benefits and should be measured separately.</p>
<p>The immediate bill can include reading the old table, writing a new copy, rebuilding indexes, temporary sorting space, log generation and competing with the live workload. In PostgreSQL, <code>VACUUM FULL</code> needs an exclusive table lock and extra disk space; <code>CLUSTER</code> likewise blocks table access and may need sorting space as well as replacement table and index storage. Online alternatives have their own concurrency, resource and final-lock conditions. Their contracts are operation- and engine-specific.</p>
<p>A rewrite earns consideration when persistent low occupancy or lost locality explains enough repeated work to repay that bill. If the same churn or long snapshots immediately recreate the problem, compaction addresses its symptom. Compare the expected useful lifetime of the improvement with its disruption and recurrence. <a href="/review/relational-next/schema-changes/">Changing schemas without breaking applications</a> owns physical DDL details; <a href="/review/relational-next/maintenance-and-upgrades/">service maintenance</a> owns scheduling, limits and recovery choices.</p>

<h2 id="partitioning-helps-only-when-it-removes-relevant-work">Partitioning helps only when it removes relevant work</h2>
<p><strong>Local table partitioning</strong> divides one logical table into child tables with declared bounds. It does not require separate servers. A query can avoid children whose bounds cannot contain a matching row: <strong>partition pruning</strong>. The useful question is which work disappears for this workload.</p>
<p>We copied the same 9,000 narrow orders into January, February and March partitions, preserving every order. The one-day report asks for 100 orders placed on 28 February.</p>
<Sql code={pruning} label="A date predicate that matches the partition bounds" />
<StorageFigure scene="pruning" number={5} />
<p>Captured PostgreSQL 18.6 execution scanned <code>orders_feb</code> only, using 32 shared hits against 103 on the original unpartitioned table. Both answers were count 100 and sum 1,000. The February scan still rejected the month’s other 2,700 rows. Pruning removed two months of page work; it did not find February’s rows individually.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL can prune during planning when values are known, and during execution for supported parameter-dependent cases. A separately captured forced-generic prepared plan reported <code>Subplans Removed: 2</code> at its Append and scanned only February, again with 32 hits. This session-only override exposes runtime pruning; it is not advice to force generic plans. Unsupported expressions or predicates that do not constrain the partition key may fail to exclude children. Check the actual plan and executed children.</p>
<p>Without a date restriction, all three children executed under an Append. That query used 104 shared hits against 103 on the original table. Partitioning also adds planning and per-child setup work; a growing partition count can make that overhead significant. These captures establish page-work changes for a small fixture, without establishing a latency or capacity benefit.</p>
<p>This evidence is enough to consider a layout, not enough to accept its lifecycle. <a href="/review/relational-next/replicas-and-partitions/">Operating replicas and partitioned data</a> owns partition-key and boundary choices, schema compatibility, future write coverage and the retain-or-reject decision. Use <a href="/review/relational-next/schema-changes/">Changing schemas without breaking applications</a> for physical DDL and <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">Changing a live database</a> for rollout and retirement gates.</p>

<h2 id="choose-a-physical-change-from-its-measured-effect">Choose a physical change from its measured effect</h2>
<p>The growing history scan has a physical explanation: wider inline notes spread identical useful results across more pages. Selecting fewer existing inline columns did not remove those visits. A narrower hot representation or a suitable index is a plausible next experiment. An index’s keys, coverage and write costs belong with <a href="/review/relational-next/indexes/">Choosing indexes for a workload</a>.</p>
<p>The other experiments lead elsewhere. Snapshot retention calls for transaction-lifetime and cleanup evidence before compaction. Reusable gaps may be absorbed by new rows. Monthly layout removed work for the bounded report and offered no page advantage to the unbounded one. None supplies a general instruction to rebuild or partition the shop’s tables.</p>
<p>Apply the parent’s comparison method with this physical evidence added: the same SQL results, heap and total allocations, page accesses and storage-I/O observations, cache conditions, version-retention horizons, reusable bytes and the intervention’s immediate write and locking costs. Distinguish warm shared-buffer hits from cold-cache loading; measure device work with appropriate interval observations rather than naming every buffer read a disk read. A proposed improvement still needs representative changes, concurrency and maintenance before deployment.</p>
<p>The native checks ran on PostgreSQL 18.6 with 8 KiB pages, 32 MB shared buffers and 4 MB work memory. They used fresh disposable databases, short bounded statements and explicit fixture-only storage and cleanup settings. No server restart, cache flush or production load test was performed. Counts establish mechanisms for these fixtures, not production timing, sustainable throughput or an external-fragmentation diagnosis.</p>
<p>To repeat them, <a href={worksheet} download="physical-storage-verify.mjs">download the native worksheet</a>. In a new working directory, install Node.js 22 or later and the driver with <code>npm install --ignore-scripts pg@8.23.1</code>. Set <code>PG_URL</code> to a disposable PostgreSQL administrative connection, then run <code>node physical-storage-verify.mjs</code>. It needs database-creation and extension privileges, creates a new <code>rel3_a24_*</code> database, saves <code>raw-results.json</code>, closes its sessions and drops that database. The default connection is the local verification instance; supply your own connection when repeating it elsewhere.</p>

